
🔥 equals() and hashCode() Contract
--------------------------------------------------
👉 It is a rule set that Java expects us to follow when
 overriding equals() and hashCode().

📜 The Contract (Very Important Points)
1. If two objects are equal → hashCode must be same ✔
a.equals(b) == true
⇒ a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()
2. If hashCode is same → objects may or may not be equal (collision allowed) ⚠️
a.hashCode() == b.hashCode()
⇒ a.equals(b) may be true or false
3. If objects are not equal → hashCode SHOULD ideally be different ✔
(not mandatory but recommended for performance)

4. Same object → always same hashCode ✔
a.equals(a) → true
a.hashCode() → same always

📦 Why this contract is important?
Because in Hash-based structures like:
HashMap
HashSet
👉 Java uses:
1. hashCode → find bucket
2. equals → check exact match
If contract breaks ❌:
Wrong bucket selection
Duplicate entries
Data not found
Unexpected behavior

“The equals and hashCode contract states that if two objects
are equal according to equals(), then their hashCode must be the same
 However, if two objects have the same hashCode, they may or may not
 be equal. This contract ensures correct behavior
 in hash-based collections like HashMap and HashSet.”

-----------------------------------------------------------
import java.util.*;

class Student {
    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int id, String name) {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    // WHY equals()?
    // 👉 used to compare TWO objects logically (data comparison)
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {

        if (this == o) return true;
        // WHY? → same memory object = directly equal

        if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
        // WHY? → ensure same type (Student vs Student only)

        Student student = (Student) o;
        // WHY? → convert Object → Student to access fields

        return id == student.id && name.equals(student.name);
        // WHY? → real data comparison
    }

    // WHY hashCode()?
    // 👉 used by HashMap to decide BUCKET location
    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hash(id, name);
        // WHY? → converts object data into a unique integer
        // same data → same hashCode → same bucket
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Map<Student, String> map = new HashMap<>();

        Student s1 = new Student(1, "Aman");
        Student s2 = new Student(1, "Aman"); // same data but different object
        Student s3 = new Student(2, "Raj");

        map.put(s1, "Java Developer");
        // STEP:
        // 1. s1.hashCode() → bucket decided
        // 2. store value in that bucket

        System.out.println("Getting with s2: " + map.get(s2));
        // STEP:
        // 1. s2.hashCode() → same bucket as s1
        // 2. equals() → true
        // 3. return value

        System.out.println("Getting with s3: " + map.get(s3));
        // STEP:
        // 1. different hashCode → different bucket
        // 2. no match → null
    }
}
